{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-148037",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{4({x}^{2}-1)(y-1)}{9y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4({x}^{2}-1)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{1}{9y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4({x}^{2}-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{9{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8x}{9{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x}{9{y}^{2}}, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
